## Decoding the question
"**Critically evaluate**" a quoted claim — weigh it, accept what is true and reject what is false, and end with a **verdict**. The question names **three bodies of evidence**: the **history of women's activism in South Asia**, **postcolonial feminist critiques** of Western feminism, and **Muslim feminist scholars**. Give each its own heading. A strong answer also admits the **grain of truth** in the claim.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Feminism** is the belief that women and men should have equal rights and opportunities, and the movement to end women's subordination. The claim that it is a **Western import** holds that it is foreign to Muslim and South Asian societies, carried in by Western ideas, donors and NGOs.

#### ii) The argument
The claim is **mostly false**. Women's rights in South Asia were argued by local reformers, Muslim scholars and women of the Pakistan Movement long before the Western "second wave", and postcolonial and Muslim feminists have built their own critiques of Western feminism. What is true is that feminism has always been part of a **global conversation**, and that some Pakistani activism has depended on Western funding and vocabulary. Feminism in Pakistan is best described as **locally rooted and globally connected**.

### 2. The claim and why it persists

#### i) Who makes it
Conservative religious parties, some media commentators and critics of the **Aurat March** argue that feminism is a "Western" or "anti-family" agenda.

#### ii) Why it persuades

##### (a) Colonial memory
British rulers used the "**status of women**" to justify colonial rule — Katherine Mayo's *Mother India* (==1927==), which Gandhi called "a drain inspector's report", is the classic example.

##### (b) Donors and NGOs
Since the 1990s much gender work in Pakistan has been **donor-funded**, giving the impression of a foreign agenda.

##### (c) Vocabulary
Terms such as "gender", "empowerment" and "bodily autonomy" arrived in English.

### 3. What is true in the claim

#### i) Global influence is real
Western feminism supplied ideas and tools: the waves, CEDAW (==1979==), the Beijing Platform (==1995==) and the language of human rights.

#### ii) Dependence is real
**NGO-isation** — professionalised, donor-funded gender projects — has sometimes made activism accountable to donors rather than to Pakistani women.

#### iii) But influence is not origin
Borrowing tools is not the same as importing the cause. Pakistani women chose, adapted and sometimes rejected Western ideas.

### 4. South Asian reform before the West's second wave

#### i) Hindu reformers
**Raja Ram Mohan Roy** campaigned against *sati*, abolished by the Bengal Sati Regulation of ==1829==; **Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar** won the **Widows' Remarriage Act** (==1856==); **Tarabai Shinde** wrote a fierce critique of male power in ==1882==.

#### ii) Laws for women
The **Age of Consent Act** (==1891==), the **Child Marriage Restraint Act** (==1929==) and the **Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act** (==1939==) were products of debates inside the subcontinent.

### 5. Muslim reformers

#### i) Writers
**Nazir Ahmad**'s *Mirat-ul-Uroos* (==1869==) promoted girls' education; **Sayyid Mumtaz Ali** wrote ***Huquq-un-Niswan*** (==1898==) and, with **Muhammadi Begum**, published *Tahzib-e-Niswan*.

#### ii) Institutions
**Sheikh Abdullah** and **Wahid Jahan Begum** founded a girls' school at **Aligarh** (==1906==); **Begum Rokeya** wrote her feminist utopia *Sultana's Dream* (==1905==); the **All-India Muslim Ladies' Conference** met from ==1914==.

#### iii) The pattern
**Reform within tradition**, with education first — a Muslim, South Asian path to women's rights.

### 6. Women of the Pakistan Movement

#### i) Political mobilisation
The Muslim League formed a **Central Women's Sub-Committee** at **Patna** in ==1938==. **Fatima Jinnah**, **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** (the only Muslim woman delegate at the Round Table Conferences), **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** and **Fatima Sughra**, who hoisted the League flag on the Punjab Civil Secretariat in ==1947==, led women into politics.

#### ii) The Quaid's view
**Muhammad Ali Jinnah** told students at Aligarh on ==10 March 1944== that "no nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you".

#### iii) The 1948 inheritance victory
Women legislators and demonstrators won the **West Punjab Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act 1948**, giving women inheritance in agricultural land — rights won by appealing to **Islam against custom**.

### 7. Activism after 1947

#### i) Welfare and reform
**APWA** (==1949==) built schools and clinics; protests after PM Bogra's second marriage led to the **Rashid Commission** and the **Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961**.

#### ii) Resistance
The **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==) challenged the **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==) and marched on ==12 February 1983==; the **Sindhiani Tehreek** (from ==1982==) mobilised rural women — hardly a Westernised elite.

::: fig timeline | Women's rights in South Asia and Pakistan — a local history.
@layout vertical
1829: Bengal Sati Regulation (Raja Ram Mohan Roy)
1856: Widows' Remarriage Act (Vidyasagar)
1898: Mumtaz Ali — Huquq-un-Niswan
1906: Aligarh girls' school
1938: Muslim League Women's Sub-Committee, Patna
1948: Punjab inheritance law — Islam against custom
1961: Muslim Family Laws Ordinance
1981: Women's Action Forum
1982: Sindhiani Tehreek
:::

### 8. Postcolonial critiques of Western feminism

#### i) Mohanty
**Chandra Talpade Mohanty**'s "**Under Western Eyes**" (==1984==) showed how Western feminism created a single, victimised "**Third World Woman**" — poor, veiled, passive — ignoring her agency and history.

#### ii) Spivak
**Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak**'s "**Can the Subaltern Speak?**" (==1988==) described colonial rule over *sati* as "white men saving brown women from brown men" — the poor woman spoken for, never heard.

#### iii) Abu-Lughod and Narayan
**Lila Abu-Lughod** asked "Do Muslim Women Really Need Saving?" (==2002==), and **Uma Narayan** (==1997==) argued that calling Third World feminism "Westernised" erases local women's own critiques.

#### iv) What this proves
The sharpest critics of Western feminism are **feminists from the South** — proof that feminism is not a single Western product.

### 9. Muslim feminist scholarship

#### i) The method
**Muslim (Islamic) feminism** seeks gender justice by **re-reading the Qur'an and Sunnah**, separating the divine message from patriarchal interpretation and custom, on the basis of ***adl*** (justice) and equal dignity.

#### ii) The scholars

##### (a) Mernissi
**Fatima Mernissi** (*The Veil and the Male Elite*, ==1987==) questioned the chains of misogynist hadith.

##### (b) Wadud and Hassan
**Amina Wadud** (*Qur'an and Woman*, ==1992==) read the Qur'an from a woman's perspective; the Pakistani-American scholar **Riffat Hassan** showed that the Qur'an does not create woman from a rib.

##### (c) Barlas
**Asma Barlas** (*"Believing Women" in Islam*, ==2002==) argued that the Qur'an is anti-patriarchal.

#### iii) Movements
**Sisters in Islam** (Malaysia, ==1988==) and **Musawah** (==2009==) campaign for equality in Muslim family law.

### 10. The limits of the counter-argument

#### i) Not every custom is authentic
Postcolonial critique can be misused to defend harmful customs — honour killing and child marriage are not "authentic culture" to be protected.

#### ii) Debate within Pakistan
Secular feminists such as **Afiya Zia** (*Faith and Feminism in Pakistan*, ==2018==) warn that relying on religious arguments limits rights; **Saba Mahmood** (*Politics of Piety*, ==2005==) argues that pious women also exercise agency. Feminism in Pakistan is itself plural.

### 11. Verdict: neither import nor pure tradition

#### i) Local roots
The history from Mumtaz Ali to WAF shows that women's rights were argued in **local and Islamic terms** before and alongside Western influence.

#### ii) Global connections
Feminism has always travelled — Pakistani activists used CEDAW, Beijing and the waves as **tools**, adapting them to local needs.

#### iii) The best description
Feminism in Pakistan is **locally rooted and globally connected** — "glocal" rather than imported.

### 12. Conclusion

#### i) The claim fails
The claim that feminism is a Western import ignores two centuries of South Asian reform, the women of the Pakistan Movement, the Muslim feminist re-reading of scripture and the postcolonial feminists who criticised the West itself.

#### ii) What remains true
It contains a warning worth heeding: activism that depends on foreign funds and borrowed language loses legitimacy. The answer is not to abandon feminism but to ground it in Pakistani history, the **Constitution** and **Islam's** principles of justice — as Pakistani women have done since 1898.

## Why this answer scores
- **Evaluates** a claim: admits its grain of truth (colonial memory, donors, NGO-isation) before rejecting it.
- Uses **all three named bodies of evidence** under separate headings, each with dated names and texts.
- Draws on **Pakistani history** (Patna 1938, Jinnah 1944, the 1948 inheritance law, WAF, Sindhiani Tehreek).
- Notes the **limits** of the counter-argument and the debate within Pakistani feminism.
- A **timeline** figure shows the local history at a glance; the verdict is clear.
